---
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

const description =
  "Simple habits for honing, washing, storing, and using your knives so their edges last longer between professional sharpenings.";
---

<ArticleLayout title="How to Care for Your Knives at Home" description={description} category="Knife care">
  <p class="article-lead">
    A well-maintained knife makes kitchen work safer and more efficient. These straightforward habits will help protect the edge and extend the time between professional sharpenings.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>1. Hone regularly</h2>
    <p>
      Honing is not the same as sharpening. A honing rod realigns the very edge of the blade; sharpening removes a small amount of metal to create a new edge. Regular honing can keep a knife feeling sharp longer.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Use a honing rod or steel that is appropriate for your knife.</li>
      <li>Work slowly and keep a consistent angle.</li>
      <li>Use light, controlled strokes on both sides of the blade.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>2. Hand wash and dry immediately</h2>
    <p>
      Dishwashers combine high heat, harsh detergent, and contact with other utensils. That treatment can damage an edge and shorten the life of the handle.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Use warm, soapy water and a soft sponge or cloth.</li>
      <li>Avoid abrasive pads and never leave a knife hidden in a sink.</li>
      <li>Dry the knife immediately to help prevent staining and rust.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>3. Store the edge safely</h2>
    <p>Loose storage can dull a knife and create a hazard when reaching into a drawer. Protect the edge with one of these options:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A clean knife block with correctly sized slots.</li>
      <li>A securely mounted magnetic strip.</li>
      <li>Individual blade guards for drawer storage.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>4. Choose the right cutting surface</h2>
    <p>
      Glass, stone, ceramic, and other very hard surfaces can quickly roll or chip an edge. Wood and quality plastic boards are much friendlier to kitchen knives. Keep the board stable with a damp towel or non-slip mat underneath.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>5. Know when honing is not enough</h2>
    <p>
      If a freshly honed knife still crushes food, slides across tomato skin, or requires extra pressure, it may need sharpening. The correct angle varies by knife, so follow the maker’s guidance when sharpening at home.
    </p>
    <div class="article-callout">
      <h3>Professional sharpening</h3>
      <p>
        A professional can restore the edge when normal maintenance no longer does the job, address minor tip damage, and recommend an edge suited to how you use the knife.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Keep the routine simple</h2>
    <p>
      Hone when needed, wash by hand, dry promptly, protect the edge in storage, and cut on a knife-friendly board. Those small habits help your knives perform better for years.
    </p>
  </section>
</ArticleLayout>
